2. Scryfall (card data and card images)
---------------------------------------

    Service:  Scryfall
    Site:     https://scryfall.com
    API docs: https://scryfall.com/docs/api
    Terms:    https://scryfall.com/docs/api  (see also /docs/api/rate-limits)

mtg-table resolves card art through the Scryfall API (tools/fetch-art.ts) and
displays card images served from cards.scryfall.io. Since 2026-09-27 it also
builds a LOCAL card library from Scryfall's data (tools/card-library/, the card
browser at #cards; DECISIONS.md D90):

  * the oracle_cards BULK file — one gzipped JSONL download (~24 MB) from
    data.scryfall.io, found through GET /bulk-data and re-downloaded only when
    Scryfall publishes a new one; plus GET /sets and, for the two sets the deck
    is built from (MSH, MSC), their prints through /cards/search (8 pages), and
    those two sets' icon SVGs, downloaded rather than hotlinked;
  * card IMAGES from cards.scryfall.io, prefetched by
    tools/card-library/images.ts for the cards in decks/ (41 images, ~3.8 MB, at
    `normal`); every print of MSH + MSC (~104 MiB) only with an explicit --yes.

Everything it downloads, and the SQLite index built from it, lives under var/cards/
(gitignored): the index, the bulk file, the set syncs, the icons and the image
cache var/cards/images/<scryfallId>/<size>[.back].<ext>. None of it is committed.

Card data and images from Scryfall are used subject to Scryfall's API terms,
which mtg-table honours as follows:

  * User-Agent: every request to api.scryfall.com sends an accurate, identifying
    User-Agent (configured as "scryfallUserAgent" in config.json) and an explicit
    Accept: application/json header. HTTP libraries are never left to choose these
    headers. The committed config.json identifies this client as
    "mtg-table/0.1 (local hobby client; github.com/jalirkan/mtg-table)"; anyone
    forking the project should put their own contact there.
  * Rate limits: requests to /cards/search, /cards/named, /cards/random and
    /cards/collection are throttled to at most 2 per second (>= 500 ms apart);
    all other API methods to at most 10 per second. HTTP 429 responses are backed
    off, honouring Retry-After. Image file origins under *.scryfall.io are not
    rate-limited but are still fetched sparingly.
  * Caching: resolved art is written once to web/public/art.json and reused, so a
    session makes no repeat API calls. Bulk endpoints are used for anything at
    scale: the card library searches its own local index (FTS5), so typing in the
    card browser sends Scryfall nothing. The bulk file is kept and re-downloaded
    only when Scryfall's /bulk-data listing names a newer one. Images are cached
    on disk keyed by Scryfall print id, size and side (DECISIONS.md D93) and a
    cached image is never fetched again; a card image that is NOT in the cache is
    loaded by the user's browser straight from cards.scryfall.io, exactly as the
    board loads its art. Nothing caches or serves images on demand: the cache is
    filled only when the user runs tools/card-library/images.ts.
  * The card browser's image route (/cards/img/<scryfallId>/<size>) exists only
    in the Vite DEV server on 127.0.0.1 and serves the user's own cache to the
    user's own browser. It never fetches: a miss is a 404, and the page then
    loads the image from cards.scryfall.io itself. It is not a proxy, and it is
    never part of a build: the card library adds nothing to web/dist/ — no
    image, no index (DECISIONS.md D92).
  * Test fixture: fixtures/cards/ commits a SLICE of Scryfall's data so the
    library's tests run offline — 172 oracle_cards lines, 84 MSH/MSC print lines
    and 6 set objects (~0.7 MB, no images), each REDUCED to the fields the library
    reads (tools/card-library/model.ts SCRY_CARD_FIELDS): no prices, no purchase
    or related URIs, no platform ids, no rulings. It is regenerated from var/cards/
    by tools/card-library/make-fixture.ts, never hand-edited. Whether even that
    slice should be committed is the user's call (DECISIONS.md D90).
  * Artist credit: wherever card art is shown, the artist name and the card's
    copyright line are shown in the same interface (card-detail panel). The card
    browser shows only whole card images (Scryfall's `normal` size), never the
    `art_crop`; every image, tile or detail, carries "Illus. <artist>" under it,
    and the detail adds the artist and the card's copyright line
    ("TM & (C) <year> Wizards of the Coast, LLC", plus "MARVEL (C) <year> MARVEL"
    for the Marvel sets, dated by the set's release, since Scryfall's data has no
    copyright field). Its top bar carries the Scryfall credit and the Fan Content
    notice, and wraps rather than clipping them. Card
    images are not distorted, skewed, stretched, blurred, sharpened, desaturated,
    colour-shifted, watermarked or overlaid with logos, and the printed copyright
    and artist credit on a card image are never cropped or covered.
  * No paywalling, no implication of Scryfall endorsement, and no bare
    repackaging or proxying of Scryfall data.

Scryfall is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast, and its own footer notes
that portions of Scryfall are unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Wizards
of the Coast Fan Content Policy.


